--- !u!114 &766005267
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 766005261}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: ede10faecdc5c7e45998ac52c146e1eb, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  description: "The Lexting UI operates via a handful of active components that work
    closely together: \n\nLexting.input (the SpatialGestureInput class): \nA singleton
    class that consolidates hand tracking input from any supported platform and interprets
    gestures relevant to the Lexting UI, routing triggers to all other components\n\nLexting.printer
    (the LextingOutputManager class): \nReceives incoming text from the WordAssemblyController
    and relays it to all listening text fields during text entry\n\nLexting.controller
    (the TwoZoneCubeController class): \nDrives the behaviors of a matrix of 2x2x2
    zone triggers for one hand, where the matrix hovers in front of and slightly
    above that hand, enabling gestures to become a desired word via simultaneous
    combos of move, rotate, grab, and grab+drag\n\nLexting.controllers (the TwoZoneCubeControllers
    class)\nThe parent object of both the left and right controller containing some
    operations that work on both at the same time.\n\nTwoZoneTrigger class: \nHandles
    behavior for one zone, usually representing a single character of the alphabet
    which can be grabbed or the letter can change based upon hand's current rotation
    state. The hovered character is available to the rest of the interface as a static
    string (currentRightHighlightedLetter & currentLeftHighlightedLetter).\n\nLexting.assembler
    (the CubeWordAssemblyController class): \nControls construction of the word that
    will be progressively filtered to match the user's desired English word as they
    grab and drag through up to four letters (two in the right hand & two in the
    left)\n\nLexting.finalizer (the TwoZoneWordListController class):\nControls finalization
    of the word provided by the word assembly controller, including final word choice
    (displayed in the left-hand UI) and punctuation (displayed in the right-hand
    UI)\n\nLexting.conjugator (the LextingConjugationState class):\nAnalyzes two-hand
    orientation patterns to provide the word list controller with a desired conjugation
    (A.K.A. suffix) during word finalization\n\nthe LextingDictionary class:\nA static
    class which the word assemly controller initializes at runtime to allocate all
    English words into a fast-lookup data structure\n\nLexting.line (the CubeDragLineVisualizer
    class): \nDisplays a rendered line between the starting zone and the currently
    highlighted zone in each hand while that hand is dragging\n\nthe HandCursor class:
    \nHandles the motion of a point cursor which always follows the hand at an offset,
    enabling the hand to activate the UI from nearby without needing to put the hand
    directly inside the UI.\n\nthe HandMarker class:\nProvides a reference to each
    hand's transform for the rest of the Lexting interface.\n\nthe HeadMarker class:\nProvides
    a reference to the head transform for the rest of the Lexting interface.\n\nAll
    of the above communicate with each other via either events of type SpatialGestureEvent,
    or directly via Singleton or Pingleton references. A Pingleton is a variation
    of a Singleton that is assigned and invoked via property name rather than class
    name so as not to interrupt inheritance. The Lexting class contains a collection
    of static quick-access shortcuts to most of these components.\n\nNOTE: UniRx
    & extension methods are utilized throughout Lexting to enable shortcuts for things
    like allocating memory. event subscription, cleaner async handling, etc. If you
    see situations where OnEnable, OnDisable, Update, or IEnumerator functions seem
    to be running even though they are nowhere to be found in code, it's because
    UniRx lets us to auto-create them all in the background case-by-case, enabling
    less superfluous abstraction and more compact classes. See https://github.com/neuecc/UniRx"
